  "slug": "protein-ribosomes-6556",
  "summary": "Recent preprints on plant imaging and species distribution modeling tie reported gains to benchmark design, sampling effort, and backbone choice.",
  "synthesis": "Across the three preprints, the evaluation protocol defining a comparison largely determines what a reported gain means. The orchid study is a controlled benchmark of Vision Transformers against CNNs for fine-grained genus identification in a data-poor flora, where self-supervised DINOv2 leads; it also releases a retrieval-based web app. SAGE pairs GBIF training records with sPlotOpen plots across 5771 plants and stratifies evaluation by sampling effort, making sampling bias an explicit axis. RootQuantV2 is more method-centric, adapting a frozen DINOv3 ViT-L/16 via parameter-efficient tuning to regress minirhizotron root length and area (R² 0.950 and 0.930). What is new is the explicit linking of conclusions to evaluation choices, though this thread is dominant rather than universal across the items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-09-27"
}
